The four clauses are the textbook 3+3+2+3 three-box inventory.

A 日/口 enclosure is top 横 + bottom 横 + left 竖 + right 竖, i.e. the “one vertical on the left, one vertical on the right’’ that closes the box. Put three enclosures together left-to-right you get the classic horizontal three-日 block:

* 一横一横再一横 = the three top 横 of the three 日 left-to-right;
* 一竖一竖再一竖 = the three left 竖 of the three 日 left-to-right;
* 一竖在左一竖在右 = the left/right framing pair that defines each 日/口 enclosure;
* 一竖一竖再一竖 = the three right 竖 of the three 日 left-to-right.

That is 日 日 日 = 晶 jīng ‘shining, crystal’, three suns side by side, the horizontal realisation of the three-box 日/田 family. 

The inventory is orientation-invariant. Rotated 90° the same verbal recipe is quoted in the literature for the three stacked 口 of 品, the vertical mirror of 晶, with 田/日 as the elementary prototype. On the wording as given, with horizontals named first and the left set of verticals then the right set of verticals, the school telling that is normally glossed is the horizontal instantiation 晶, 品 being its well-known vertical analogue. 冊 and a single 田/日 enclosure do not give the required 3+3+2+3 repetition.

